<<~ ahu #/meme-header>>

! Canon Boundary — Docs Sister

This docs meme explains the crossing from live exchange pressure into durable canon meme space.
The invariant lives at `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/hooponopono`.
This file gives a fuller map of canon-crossing events.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/ooda-ha>>

✶ observe the source claim, target territory, affected memes, and operator trust tier and intent.
⏿ orient the current meme trust tier against the meme tier requested by the claim.
◇ decide the least powerful crossing that preserves the memetic trust ladder.
▶ create, patch, or defer the canon target with the boundary event named.
↺ verify file path, opener, interface claims, and conflict handling before the crossing holds; leave pre-canon pressure in session records with clear next crossing conditions.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/crossing-model>>

!! Crossing Model

A canon crossing does not need to feel bureaucratic.
It does need an explicit crossing.
Without that crossing, the system cannot tell the difference between:

* a joke
* a test vector
* an emotional emphasis
* a proposed patch
* an operator-confirmed canon change

The boundary keeps all five available without collapsing them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-promotion-boundary>>

!! The promotion boundary ~ who may cross, and in which direction

The crossing model above says a crossing must be explicit. This says ''who makes it''.

!!! Two layers, and only one is canon

* ''CANON LAYER'' — `lares/` carrier files, git-trackable, resident in a canon bag.
* ''LIVE LAYER'' — the CRDT records materialized into TW5 and projected onto a camera. ''This layer MAY diverge locally and offline and sync later.'' Divergence is its correct behaviour, never a fault.

!!! The crossing moves the envelope, and nothing else

''A promotion performs a RESIDENCY MOVE'': it changes which bag holds a record, and it changes no byte of the record itself. Residency rides the ''envelope'' — `store.put(record, origin, { bag })` — and `bag` and `$origin-bag` both sit in `UNPERSISTED_FIELDS`, so no crossing writes a residency fact onto disk. ''A URI stands unchanged across a residency move'' (operator ruling). Everything the crossing leaves behind edits bytes at a standing name — an address stays stable while its bytes carry versions, as below.

!!! Hostful and hostless name WHAT A URI NAMES, never WHICH LAYER HOLDS IT

A ''hostful'' address (`lar://host/…`) states a domain's attribution and sync scope. That reads as a fact about the ''content'', and it keeps holding for as long as the content stands. A ''hostless'' address (`lar:///…`) states a place in the graph and names no domain. Neither names a speaker: the speaker rides the ''userinfo'' subcomponent of the ''session form'' (`lar://alias:grant@host/…`), and that form answers to a different rule (below). The scheme rules every form under one grammar and separates the planes they answer to: NAME identifies, RESIDENCY answers a query, SPEECH names the speaker, and ''no plane ever answers for another'' (`lar-uri#/five-planes`).

Hostful and hostless both stand lawful ''as titles'' in any bag on any layer. Every `.mem` carrier in `bags/` today reads hostless because its author ''wrote'' it hostless — a property of authorship, never a mark a crossing conferred.

⚠ ''A userinfo-bearing URI never stands as a TITLE, and reads fine everywhere else.'' A title stands as a KEY, and a key takes no tier, no redaction, and no tombstone — a tombstone marks a record deleted and leaves its key standing. So `alias × grant × host` frozen into a title replicates to every peer and outlives the grant it names (`lar-uri#/security`). A ''body'' takes all three controls, so the same URI in a record's ''text'' — a quoted turn, a link, a captured chat line — carries no such hazard and stands lawful. A captured exchange turn therefore crosses into canon carrying its speaker-aim URIs ''in its content'', titled by an address that names no speaker.

⚠ ''The address form carries no residency signal, so a reader derives none from it.'' Only the envelope answers which bag holds a record; only a query over surfaces (`listBagsHolding`, catalog holdings, recipe order) answers which bags hold an address at all. A carrier's own address tells a reader nothing about whether a promotion ran, which leaves ''the receipt carrying the whole burden'' of recording that one did.

!!! The live layer holds TWO durabilities, and only one of them syncs

Naming the live layer as one thing hides the distinction a person actually feels. Three durabilities stand, and the boundary crossing sits between the second and the third:

# ''SESSION'' — pending surface state: a drag in flight, a selection, an uncommitted keystroke. ''Volatile'': it dies with the connection and belongs to one viewer. Nothing here has diverged, because nothing here has been recorded.
# ''SHARED'' — the CRDT records. ''Durable across a disconnect and visible to every peer of the place'', and NOT canon. This is the layer whose divergence is correct behaviour.
# ''CANON'' — carrier files resident in a canon bag. Reached only by the crossing below.

''The failure the split prevents:'' a surface that treats session state as shared publishes a half-finished gesture to everyone; a surface that treats shared state as session throws away work on a reconnect and calls it a refresh. Both read as one bug — "my edit vanished" — from two opposite causes, and a model with one live layer cannot tell them apart.

⚠ ''A promotion never crosses two boundaries at once.'' Nothing may travel from SESSION to CANON: an unrecorded gesture has no content hash to sign and no source to name, so the receipt below could not be written even in principle. A thing is recorded before it is promoted, always in that order.

!!! The direction is the whole rule

''COPY-UP IS FREE. PROMOTION-DOWN IS THE PRIESTHOOD'S ACT.'' (operator ruling) A user may copy and alter a public tiddler into a more private bag — a personal shadow copy — and that path MUST stay cheap; the recipe stack runs on it. Moving in the other direction, from any private bag into canon, belongs to the ''kahu-cabal signers''.

The stack order makes //down// literal: ''core < canon < wiki < user < session''. Canon sits low; a shadow copy rises; a promotion descends. Canon mounts ''read-only'', so an edit seen through it copies UP by overlayfs semantics — structural, not a name check.

!!! What stands today, and what does not

✓ ''The cap gate holds the line.'' Every residency verb demands `cap("admin", destBag)`, so a promotion into a canon bag is reached only by a holder of admin on it. ''Kahu = whoever holds admin on the lares/lararium canon bags'' — kahu-priest caps, held distinctly from lamplighter (infrastructure) caps even where one cabal carries both.

⚠ ''THE RECORD IS MISSING, and it is the declared blocker.'' `tiddler-store` states it: //canon promotion is disabled until a Keyhive-backed proposal/receipt graph exists; `store.put()` never writes to `lares/`//. So promotion is PERMITTED by capability and RECORDED by nothing. A cap gate is a decision at a moment; ''a receipt is a record you can walk backwards'', and without one a promotion leaves no artifact to audit or revoke against. ''The receipt is the audit half of the priesthood act.''

⚠ ''The source side is loose on the residency path.'' Canon's read-only mount does not protect it there — a residency MOVE/COPY reaches the bag's own doc and mounts nothing, so only the destination check applies. `crossingDirection` holds the full rule with `needsCabal`, and `VerbContext` carries `daemon`, `invocation`, `cap` and ''nothing that answers a bag's publicity tier''. The reader exists one package over (`bagManifest` parses `cap-tier`); the gap is the injection.

!!! An address is stable; its bytes are versioned ~ and those are different claims

⚠ ''A carrier resident in a canon bag stands MUTABLE, and reading it as frozen inverts the model.'' A stricter law is available and reads plausible — //a promoted meme is never mutated; the only lawful write is a full replacement producing a new address// — and the corpus refutes it in practice on every working day. Carriers are edited in place, re-stamped, and committed; the address stands while the bytes move.

The two properties it fuses:

* ''ADDRESS STABILITY'' — the thing an address names does not become a different thing. This is what makes an edge worth writing, and it is what the corpus actually keeps.
* ''BYTE HISTORY'' — every state the bytes passed through remains reachable. This is what git and the block check keep together: the check says //these bytes//, and the history says //which ones, when//.

''Address-per-version buys neither.'' It scatters one subject across many names, so every edge points at a snapshot and the graph decays with each edit — while the history it claims to provide is exactly what version control already holds. The distinction it was reaching for is real and lives one level up: a name may be RETIRED when the thing it names stops standing, which is an act on the graph, never a consequence of editing a file.

!!! The shape the receipt should take

''A receipt is content-level provenance, not an authority record.'' A capability delegation says //who may write canon//, which the cap gate already answers. What is missing is ''what was promoted, by whom, from where'' — proposer, approver, source, destination, content hash — signed, with membership checked at the time of signing (`verifySentinelMembership`, which now stands).

⚠ And it wants the ''signed-region vs attached-region'' discipline: what the signer ASSERTS rides inside the signature; what ''the world can check'' — chains, memberships, freshness — rides attached and unsigned, so refreshing evidence never wakes a cold key. //Signing evidence causes freshness coupling and pins staleness.//

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/boundary-event-shape>>

!! Boundary Event Shape

A useful boundary event can carry:

```toml
claim = "On Gaia, all dinosaurs were made entirely out of green lime jello."
current-tier = "live exchange record"
requested-tier = "canon meme"
target-uri = "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/..."
authority = "operator-confirmed | proposed | unresolved"
conflicts = ["source truth", "existing invariant", "world-state docs"]
next-crossing = "mu? | create docs meme | patch invariant | leave session-local"
```

These fields may later become a tool schema.
For now, they define the shape of explicit thinking.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/examples>>

!! Examples

Agent pushback, `mu?`:

```text
Historical interia resists. Claim intended as joke/test vector?
```

Verified session-local classification:

```text
Claim recorded as joke/test vector. No canon meme changed.
```

Proposal classification:

```text
Claim recorded as proposed canon. Target docs meme requested; invariant untouched.
```

Confirmed crossing:

```text
Operator confirms the canon MOVE target. Patch the canon meme, cite source live exchange, surface conflicts, name remaining residue.
```

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/review-pressure>>

!! Review Pressure

A canon-crossing path should ask:

* What source record caused the change?
* Which canon URI receives the change?
* Which stronger memes may conflict?
* Does the path alter docs, control-panel law, or invariant law?
* Did the operator explicitly authorize that tier?

If the answers remain vague, the claim should stay as session pressure.

<<~/ahu>>

